   I'm not convinced that all Randians have a similar understanding of it. 
   At once point I thought it was synonymous with 'reality exists', which
   I accept as a tautology or definition of reality [reality = that which
   exists], but I have since been told that Rand distinguished the two
   statements - unfortunately my source couldn't remember the distinction!

<our intrepid hero girds on her flame-retardant Hero Outfit (TM) and
steps unhesitatingly (and more-or-less boldly) into the fray>

I think that "existence exists" is the tautology.  For "reality
exists" to be the tautology would narrow the definition of reality.
To start:  my (American Heritage) dictionary defines "existence" as
"the fact or state of existing; being" (among other definitions, none
of which include "real" or "reality" -- though to be fair "exist" is
defined as "to have actual being; be real" (I contend this is in one
of the narrower senses of "real)).

On the other hand, "reality" is defined as "the quality or state of
being actual or true", and as "the totality of all things possessing
actuality, existence, or essence" ("true" and "essence" being the
words that I find interesting here).  For example, "The reality is
that he loves you.".  I would say that love is real, but not that love
exists, and in general that feelings/events occur or do not occur
(thus giving them essence/reality) but not that they exist.

Reality is also defined (as a Philosophy technical term) as "that
which has necessary existence and not contingent existence".  These
three definitions together imply that the term "reality" covers both
too much (in some senses, as in the first two definitions) and too
little (in other senses, as in the last definition) to replace
"existence" in "existence exists". 

   Perhaps it is time to examine the statement 'existence exists' from
   another angle.  Consider the negation of 'existence exists', or as I
   prefer 'reality exists', which would be 'reality doesn't exist'.  There
   are lots of people who would superficially agree with this statement -
   for instance some Hindus believe the world is an illusion in the mind
   of Vishnu, the preserver (if this isn't true, substitute Hindu for your
   favourite flaky religion/belief/quantum philosophy/mumbo-jumbo).  But
   what do they mean by 'reality' in this case?  That reality is subsumed
   and contained within Vishnu's mind.  When they hear us use the word
   'reality' they are assuming that we mean the world as it appears to our
   senses.  What they mean by reality is the 'higher' reality of
   Vishnu/Brahma/Shiva.  Thus they are not denying that 'reality exists'
   but rather that 'reality is as we perceive'.  It would seem that
   negating 'reality exists' is fruitless.  Upon examination everyone
   accepts that reality exists, so the reaction has to be, so what?

Sorry to quote all that, but I'm not sure how to break it up.  I don't
think Hindu mumbo-jumbo is necessary.  For one thing, taking "reality"
as "the totality of all that is real", the negation of "reality
exists" can be, not "reality does not exist", but "not all reality
exists", which follows from my first two definitions and example
above.

The negation of "existence exists" is harder (thus, more interesting,
in my view).  Taking "existence" to be "the totality of all that
exists" the negation of "existence exists" would be "the totality of
all that exists does not exist" or "not all the totality of all that
exists exists".  These look like contradictions to me, though they
could be interpreted in a Zen-like way, in which to say that "the
totality of all that exists exists" would be limiting the totality, like
saying "the set of all numbers is a number" (though, if I interpret
it well at all, I think the Zen teaching is more like "the-totality-of-
all-that-exists-and-all-that-does-not-exist both exists and does not
exist" -- apologies to followers of Zen who are clenching their teeth
and reaching for their keyboards at this point).

