Date: Sat, 25 Sep 93 12:54:09 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: RIGHTS: "Right of Privacy," PPLs, and Schelling Points

Dave Burns has been asking me in e-mail questions about my general support
for Mark Miller's comments in "Extropy" about the "Lotus Marketplace"
decision. Recall that Mark felt the decision to withdraw "Marketplace" was
wrong-headed and a "blow to freedom," or somesuch.

I agree with Mark, for reasons I'll mention below.

Dave asked me about my definition of "rights," which I used as shorthand in
a post, and I answered, albeit tersely (I can write long-windedly for lists
and newsgroups, but find myself lacking in energy/will to write long
letters that I know will be seen by one person and then be lost forever.
Sosumi.).

Dave has asked for more details, which I'd rather be intended for a post to
the List than for e-mail to him. As I haven't gotten permission to quote
Dave's latest e-mail to me, I'll just excise his comments completely.
Here's hoping my comments make sense.

.....

Lotus withdrawing "Marketplace" for business reasons is admittedly not
"tyrannical," but Lotus withdrawing it because of an implied threat of
lawsuits over privacy matters _is_ verging on tyranny.

(As if someone came up to me and said, "Tim, I hear you're saving copies of
e-mail and compiling "reputation records" for sale to other people. You
might want to reconsider that, else I'll have the DA poking around and an
expensive lawsuit, which you'll lose.")

...comments from Dave about why this debate is of interest to him, but not
to me, at least not to the same extent....

Sure, it's your area of interest. To me, the answers are already fairly
clear, especially as buttressed by Benson in his "The Enterprise of Law."

I choose to concentrate in the areas I mostly post on.


...Dave asks why it is OK for talk of hiring third parties (like protection
agencies in an anarchocapitalist/PPL system) for *physical protection*, but
not for *privacy protection*.

It's up to people to protect their own privacy. If they have a birthmark,
say, they don't want publicized, they can cover it up as best they can. If
they show up on a beach in a bikini and I see the birthmark, I "own" the
results of my observation. I can write about it in my diary, put it in a
database (even like Lotus Marketplace, on CD-ROM!), and probably even
photograph it ("probably" because this is hazier...I don't claim to have
all the answers here). (My views are here even more extreme than is the
norm, as I don't believe in the "public figure" distinction. But that fact
that so many other people support some laws against photographing people in
public, or writing about them in magazines, suggests the Schelling point
"right" may not be where I would otherwise place it.)

The "pragmatic" issue I raised earlier (that abortion laws are essentially
unenforceable except with costly, intrusive, police states, while
infanticide laws are generally easily enforceable...and that this
establishes a "market" test for "abortion rights") applies as follows to
this birthmark example:

- any "third party" which attempted to enforce the so-called "privacy
rights" of the individual who exposed her birthmark to all, would have to
have police state powers to inspect diaries, journals, collections of
photographs taken at the beach, etc. (Looser enforcement, such as barring
publishing in magazines, is possible. Even here I would take the
anarchocapitalist position: those who don't want to see Lady Di's
birthmark, or who believe she has some "right" to privacy even though she
wore a bikini on a public beach, are perfectly free to avoid such magazines
and tabloids, and to make clear to the publisher their reasons for doing
so.)

- enforcement is generally too costly, even for *real* police states! Even
in China, a claim to the local gendarmerie that one's neighbor is compiling
illegal observations about neighbors would be met with a yawn. (My example
may have seemed too other-worldly, then, but suppose someone really did put
this kind of information in a database? Was FDR's well-hidden polio a real
secret? In many Communist countries, publishing nearly any information
about *leaders* is punishable. The real effect of most "privacy laws" is to
protect the entrenched.)

Scenario: I have private database files on many people (I actually do, as I
sort mail, snippets of info, addresses, etc. into a database...when I get
around to it). This is *inside the walls of my house*.

Under no PPL/customary law system I have seen explicated by either Benson
or Friedman, would a "third party" be allowed entrance into my house to
inspect my files, my personal journal, my reading material, my refrigerator
contents, my selection of video tapes, and so on. Thus, my private files
are "unreachable" to outsiders except by "police state" measures.

(Another way of viewing this: society would look at the implications of,
say, Microsoft being able to do no-knock searches in the middle of the
night, looking for copyright violations or incorrectly filled-in
registration cards, and would scream bloody murder. A few Microsofties
would get shot dead, and the shooters would be not be prosecuted (by other
protection agencies). (I'm skirting over details, but you know them.)
Ultimately, then, a system in which Microsoft can enter homes or businesses
a la the BATF would not be stable...Microsoft would have to pay too much to
its protection agencies, and it would back down, finding other ways to
protect its intellectual property...another topic for discussion.)

And once I have records I can keep secret at my residence, for example, I
can sell them using various crypto schemes. Crypto-anarchy and all that
good stuff. This cannot be stopped, short of police state measures to ban
secret messages, as discussed so often here.

So, hiring "third parties" to protect privacy seems grossly unworkable, and
in a Friedman/Benson sense, would simply carry too high a cost. Protection
agencies would refuse contracts that required them to try--emphasis on
try--to enter private dwellings and businesses. (Maybe businesses would
allow it under some conditions, as reciprocal deals can be made. And
surprise visits by OSHA and other inspectors are common in today's world
and don't cause _too much_ ruckus. The "copyright police" even do this...a
disturbing trend might be the Software Publishers Association showing up at
private residences.)


I suspect the "stable solution" is that certain basic behaviors/actions are
"protectable"--call them "rights" for want of an adequate word--and others
are not.

Western civilization settled on a handful of basic ideas, namely, that
murder, theft, trespass, etc. are actions which will be "actionable" (by
one's neighbors, the community, the State, etc....way back, it was fairly
clear the interests of these groups were quite similar, as I suspect even
Lysander Spooner would've agreed).

It also emerged that _other_ actions, such as "thinking bad thoughts," or
"reading banned books," carried too high an enforcement cost--their
language may've been different, but the idea was similar--and that the
stable solution, the workable solution, was to not have laws against
"thinking bad thoughts." A Schelling point agreed upon by most
participants.

"We hold these truths to be self-evident," and all that jive.

I could enumerate the "rights" of freedom of assembly, freedom of speech,
etc., but this is familiar territory. I don't think many people actually
believe these "rights" come from God, or Baal, or Yog-Sothoth Himself.
Rather, they emerge as the most stable set of ideas for a community to
operate on. Schelling points, as above.

Not perfect, and under attack the last century or so, but fairly stable. In
a Darwin/Dawkins sense, these memes survive and flourish. That "open
societies" are emerging around the world, and are the norm in many domains
(friendships, mailing lists, books, magazines, markets), says something
important about the staying power of the basic ideas.

Many of us would expect that a system of PPL agents would evolve to roughly
the same set of "rights." To wit, 

* freedom of speech, because it costs too much to pay to silence people on
a large scale. Cheaper to just let them babble. (If a few PPL agencies
tried to limit speech, other agencies would dispute this, and so
on...eventually, the reasoning goes, freedom of speech would largely be the
norm. This is not to say certain communities, or religious groups, could
not enforce on their members speech codes, etc. This already happens--this
List is such an example.)

* freedom of assembly, because the cost of telling people who they meet
with, what places they can go, is too high. (Trespass is covered by
"property rights," another emergent "Schelling point." Property is fairly
easily defended, and doesn't require imposing rules on large numbers of
folks in distant locations...only those who trespass.)

* And so on.

(I don't claim, by the way, that all of these rights are fully
self-evident--whatever that means--or that all rights will be recognized by
all people (they obviously won't be), or even that they are really
"rights." I'm using the term "right" as shorthand, as Perry M. did a while
back. And I certainly think the term "right" has gotten abused, as when
people talk about "everyone's right to a good job," the "right" to health
care, the "right" not to have one's feelings hurt by white male computer
programmers, etc.)

So, hiring third party protection agencies to enforce "privacy rights" is
generally unworkable, as it requires entering private homes, businesses,
etc., and inspecting private files, records, diaries, etc. Judge Bork was
likely right when he said there is no intrinsic right of privacy enumerated
anywhere in the Constitution.

Saying there is an enforceable right to privacy is analogous to saying that
in PPL system we can hire "third parties" to enforce our own reading
habits, hiring rent-a-cops to enter people's homes to make sure they don't
have any copies of "Science and Sanity" laying around, hiring goons to
seize copies of "The Satanic Verses" from beachgoers, etc.

Sure, someone could _try_ this, for a while. A lot of them would get shot.
Eventually, they'd stop such nonsense. (When the State steps in, all bets
are off, as this is a "metastable" state in which the wishes and desires of
the population, the folks who would otherwise have to support rights with
their pocketbooks, are overridden. Thus, the State of Iran can forbid
alcohol on pain of death, even though a huge number of Iranians use to
drink alcohol, and probably still do.)

But talking about this would be a digression into why and how states can
acquire powers that the marketplace would not otherwise provide. Friedman
covers this in detail in "The Machinery of Freedom."

Anarchy works. It's how we run most of our lives: relationships with
friends and family, with bookstores and grocery stores, and with others we
encounter as strangers are largely run _anarchically_. No real laws, except
the most basic "Schelling points" of not killing them, not interfering with
them physically, etc. Other than that, reputations matter, repeat business
matters, and stable solutions usually emerge.


Getting back to Dave's original questions to me about my support of the
Lotus Marketplace CD-ROM position Mark Miller took, here's my take on the
situation:

- if Lotus used information obtained by force by the government (tax
records, census data, etc.), then this was "fruit of the poisoned tree" and
should not have been made available to _anyone_, including Lotus. (This
would be analogous in the birthmark example to the government requiring we
strip for them so they could take photos, which they'd then sell to the
highest bidder for inclusion in a database. An act of aggression has taken
place.)

- if Lotus merely used publically-observable data, perhaps published in
some other form elsewhere or compiled by them by noncoercive means, then
putting in on a CD-ROM is just a _service_, like publishing a book.

- that people are "uncomfortable" with a "database on CD-ROM" is their
business, but is just their personal preference, like what they read or
watch.

- if they wish to boycott Lotus, as many threatened to do, then this is
fine. Lotus may decide to withdraw the product, for market reasons.

- if they get a false sense of security (Mark's point that the data remains
available to many, just not to consumers like us), then too bad! (As a
Neo-Calvinist, I think they get what they deserve.)

- if threats of legal action against Lotus were applied--as I gather they
were--then this is "wrong" (by the agreed-upon minimum standards I support,
as discussed above).


Talking about, for example, hiring third party protection agencies to stop
Lotus, for example, is the *wrong* approach. (Not the talk, but actually
doing it.) 

No different from hiring goons to enter bookstores to remove copies of "The
Satanic Verses."

I hope this clarifies my position.


--Tim May

