Extropians,

Perry has characterizes the "Names" of Lloyds of London as being
unwilling or unable to accept losses for which they had accepted
the risks.  I believe the situation was otherwise.  What happened
to Lloyds could be symbolized by one word:  Asbestos.

Re-insurance is a Lloyds mainstay.  Insurance companies around
the world routinely reduce their risk by re-insuring with pools
formed from among Lloyds Names.  In effect, they take out
policies that will cover them if they take a big hit from an
earthquake or a hurricane.

What happened to Lloyds was the tail end of a chain reaction
which began with activist/interventionist judges in the United
States.  In recent years, American judges have increasingly taken
it upon themselves to abrogate contracts in the name of "social
good" or "fairness."  In the case of contractual liability for
injuries cased by asbestos and toxic chemicals, the courts have
re-written insurance contracts, ex post facto, to achieve
outcomes unanticipated by the contracting parties.  What this has
meant is tremendous extensions of insurers' liabilities beyond
which they had planned for in their actuarial computations.
American insurance companies have taken massive hits for often
dubious injuries occurring decades past they time for which they
had contracted to be liable.

They, in turn, passed their losses on to their re-insurers--the
Names of Lloyds.  Since Names have unlimited liability, many
faced financial ruin.

The question this raises in my mind is: Is it reasonable to
expect the Names to quietly continue to play by the rules, when
men in robes a third of the way around the world are unilaterally
changing the rules against the Names?  I think not.  Though the
Names could be characterized as sore losers, I think it is more
useful to see viewed as what they are--victims of the State.

