Date: Sat, 25 Sep 1993 18:25:16 -0400
From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: Meaninglessness

I have rearranged Mike's post and elided some parts, I hope without
changing his meanings.

At 12:17 PM 9/25/93 +0000, Michael Clive Price wrote:
>I am grateful for Dave Burns persevering with our dialogue, we are
>understanding each other more.

I, in turn, am gratified at your willingness to discuss something at length
that I'm not sure you were particularly interested in originally.

There seem to be three issues:

1) whether operationalism excludes subjectivity and judgment, and

2) if not, what it does exclude, and

3) whether understanding can occur in the absence of meaning.

Mike says:
>Subjective opinions are fine and admissible as evidence. 
>Science works by consensus.  

This was not my understanding, but as I have no qualifications in this area
and that was not central to my question about meaning, I'm willing to go on
Mike's word. So strike off issue 1.

My understanding of Mike's position is that operationalism excludes talk
about concepts that are non-operational, but that persons can build up
elaborate discourses (classical philosophy?, art?, etc.) and shared
understandings based upon non-operational concepts. Although x may say
something to y, and y thinks she understands it, it may be meaningless.

So my objection boils down to two questions: 
        Is 'meaningful' just a synonym of 'operational?'
        How we can get a repeatable procedure for excluding weak thinking
that is not simply one person arbitrarily declaring another's statements
meaningless? 
       
My initial answers are, (meaningful and operational are distinct, both in
my  experience talking to people and in my dictionary), and (I don't know).
Is the procedure a challenge - 'Tim*, I challenge you to provide
operational definitions of concepts x, y, and z?' What are the prospects
for this procedure, how many false positives and false negatives can we
expect?

>> I'm claiming that by your criteria, "novel" and "Picasso's Blue
>> period" are meaningless. If you think they are defined operationally,
>> I don't understand at all.
>
>I don't understand the problem with "novel (book)".  Due you mean the
>subjective evaluation about a novel's content?  When is a novel not a
>novel?  

I meant, hand someone an object, and ask them, 'is this a novel?' Some
things definitely are, some things definitely aren't, and others inhabit
borderline regions, so that different subjects might respond differently.
Also, a literary critic would use the same word differently from a truck
driver. The category described by that word is not sharply, operationally
defined. (But this has to do with issue 1, which is no longer in dispute.)

>If by "Picasso's Blue period" you mean 

I mean does it pass the operational test, since not all its applications
are uncontroversial. If I found a Picasso in my attic, there might be some
doubt among experts as to whether it was from his 'blue period' or not. It
might have the characteristics of that period, but was executed before or
after the accepted times, or was at the right time but the wrong style, or
something else. The works of Picasso that come from the blue period have
certain family resemblances (some quite objective, as in the paints used,
some not) without any necessarily decisive characteristic. Again, this is
issue 1, no longer in dispute. If expert testimony is accepted as
operational, in spite of professional controversies, then no problem.

>> So [Newton's] position on the qualities of bodies is clear. How 
>> about philosophy and theology?
>
>His premises (a belief in the bible and the wisdom of the Ancients)
>being flawed meant that his conclusions (Arianism, astrology and
>alchemy) were flawed.

Doesn't that mean that he disagreed with you (and me) on where to draw the
line between things that need to be operationalized and things that don't?

(Cop out alert!-) If I am mistaken on Einstein and Newton, I have a
physical chemist, Michael Polanyi, to blame. One physicist tells me one
thing, another tells me another. Both cite their professional literature,
of which I am innocent. Poor me!

>> ... it's not obvious to me how to apply operationalism to the 
>> question of identity through change, etc.
>
>Me neither, it's a tough nut to crack.  

I was using that (one of the few famous problems of metaphysics I recall
off hand) as an example. If there's been any metaphysics in our discussion,
operational or otherwise, I didn't notice it (but my knowledge of
philosophy is spotty).

>We'd have to start by defining
>a test for identity.  I can't think of one that I'm completely happy
>with.

If subjectivity is okay, why not just get someone off the street, have them
watch me change something, then ask them, 'Is this still the same thing?'
See, that's the source of discomfort I have with Mike's inclusion of
subjective data under operationalism. The experimenter might as well just
do everything by introspection. There has to be at least a preference for
'objective' tests which economize on the use of human judgment, or you
won't get repeatability. Or is that the key? Use as much subjectivity as
you like, but you only have good results if it's repeatable?

>Perry would often get into an
>argument with someone who would say "I think strangling kittens is
>morally wrong" or whatever.  Whereupon Perry would challenge them to
>define what they meant by 'right' and 'wrong'.  Can't remember anyone
>ever being able to coherently rise to the challenge, because the concept
>of absolute right and wrong is not capable of an operational definition.

If the dictionary definition isn't good enough, I wonder that Perry doesn't
use a variation of this tactic in every argument. If they come up with a
definition, ask for a definition of subsidiary concepts used in the
definition, then lather, rinse, and repeat. There are no primitives in
natural language, so if Perry limits his victim to non-circular definitions
there will always be an undefinable object. Natural language is not built
up from fundamental definitions to complex definitions as in mathematics,
it is a pragmatic, interconnected network of interdependent meanings.

>What experiment can you perform to test whether eating fish on Fridays
>is a sin?  

(None a true extropian can perform, but a fellow Catholic could select a
sample of test subjects and a control group, have some eat fish on Friday
and some not, then count heads when they get to the afterlife. Be sure to
control for acts of contrition!-)

>Yet many people believe in absolute right and wrong and talk
>to each other about it (at great length).

I still think they could pass my test for meaning. Have two theologians
teach theology to two groups of subjects, then have the subjects
'translate' a theological discussion between the two theologians. I think
you would get repeatable results. Same with the Randians, though we can't
say for sure without performing the experiment. Whether they're right,
whether they understand each other, and whether they use operational
concepts are separate issues. Which one (or what else) counts as meaning?

>two lunatics who were convinced they could speak
>Venusian (click bang whistle etc) and introduced them to each other (on
>TV, I think), expecting denunciations and confusion.  Whereupon they
>chatted merrily away, convinced they understood each other.  

Seems to me the test is not whether they think they understand each other,
but whether both would provide similar translations of their conversations.


